# .mutation-allowlist - surviving (LIVED) mutants accepted as already known.
#
# SCOPE: the packages inside the mutation gate scope (see MUTATE_EXCLUDE in the
# Makefile): internal/config, internal/store, and the internal/ui root files.
# Mutations outside that scope are not run at all, so they can never appear here.
#
# Format:  <MUTATOR> <file>:<line>
#
# Add an entry ONLY when the mutant is provably equivalent (no test can ever kill
# it, because the mutation does not change observable behaviour) or the gap is a
# conscious, accepted risk. Adding a real test gap here hides a real risk, and
# mutation testing exists precisely to surface those.
#
# Entries are keyed by LINE NUMBER, so inserting code above an entry shifts it
# and will surface a new red on purpose: re-verify the gap and update this file,
# do not regenerate it wholesale (that would silently re-hide real gaps).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every entry below is a PROVABLY EQUIVALENT MUTANT: the replacement is
# observationally identical to the original for every possible input, so no test
# can kill it. Each one states why. Do not add real test gaps here.
#
# Negated guards deserve extra care: CONDITIONALS_NEGATION flips WHICH BRANCH
# RUNS, so "equivalent" means the newly-taken branch is a proven no-op, not that
# the removed branch was the last statement. query_history.go:193 was briefly
# parked here on exactly that mistake; the mutant swallowed a failed rename.
#
# The clamp family (if x > N { x = N }, if x < N { x = N }) has one recurring
# shape here: the boundary mutant fires at equality and the body assigns the
# value already present, so it is a no-op. That accounts for every entry in
# group A below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# === Group A: clamp boundaries, body assigns the value already present =====

# internal/config/keybindings_groups.go:123 - `if n < lo` -> `if n <= lo` (digitRange min).
# Fires only at n == lo, where the body `lo = n` is a no-op.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/config/keybindings_groups.go:123

# internal/config/keybindings_groups.go:126 - `if n > hi` -> `if n >= hi` (digitRange max).
# Mirror of the entry above: fires only at n == hi, where `hi = n` is a no-op.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/config/keybindings_groups.go:126

# internal/config/scanner.go:47 - `projects[i].Path < projects[j].Path` -> `<=`, inside
# the `!=` guard. The two forms differ only on equal strings, and the guard sends
# equal paths to the Name comparison instead.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/config/scanner.go:47

# internal/config/scanner.go:214 - `a.project.Path < b.project.Path` -> `<=`, the
# lexicographic tiebreak after the equal-length guard. Only equal strings could
# distinguish them, and two candidates in a group that shared a path would be
# the same FoundProject, so the winner is unobservable either way.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/config/scanner.go:214

# internal/store/query_history.go:76 - `len(s.entries) > 500` -> `>= 500`. At exactly
# 500 the mutant slices entries[0:], which is the same 500 entries.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/store/query_history.go:76

# internal/store/query_history.go:87 and :101 - `i < j` -> `i <= j` in the All() and
# Favorites() reversal loops. The extra iteration runs only once the pointers
# meet, and it swaps the middle element with itself.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/store/query_history.go:87
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/store/query_history.go:101

# internal/ui/modal.go:119 and :122 - the modal width clamps.
#   119: `if modalW < 50` -> `<= 50`, fires only at modalW == 50, body sets 50.
#   122: `if modalW > 90` -> `>= 90`, fires only at modalW == 90, body sets 90.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:119
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:122

# internal/ui/modal.go:194 - `if maxVisible < 5` -> `<= 5`. Fires only at
# maxVisible == 5, where the body sets 5.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:194

# internal/ui/modal.go:198 - `if m.scroll > len(lines)-maxVisible` -> `>=`. Fires only
# at exact equality, where the body re-assigns the same value. NOTE: this line's
# INVERT_NEGATIVES and ARITHMETIC_BASE mutants are NOT listed: they are real gaps
# and are killed by TestHelpModal_ScrollInsideTheGapStillShowsFullWindow. Only the
# boundary form is equivalent.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:198

# internal/ui/modal.go:201 and :206 - `end` and `scroll` clamps. `scroll < 0` -> `<= 0`
# and `end > len(lines)` -> `>= len(lines)`, both firing only at equality.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:201
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:206

# internal/ui/modal.go:71 and :88 - the scroll-above-zero guards in the ctrl+u
# handler and the wheel-up branch. `m.scroll < 0` -> `<= 0`, a no-op at 0.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:71
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:88

# internal/ui/modal.go:86 - `if msg.Y < 0` -> `if msg.Y <= 0` in the wheel branch.
# A wheel event with Y == 0 is not a scroll, and the mutant routes it to the
# scroll-up branch where `m.scroll--` then clamps back to 0, so the observable
# scroll value is unchanged. Every other wheel value is unaffected.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/modal.go:86

# internal/ui/toast.go:130 and :133 - the toast width clamps.
#   130: `if needed < toastMinWidth` -> `<=`, a no-op at the minimum.
#   133: `if needed > toastMaxWidth` -> `>=`, a no-op at the maximum.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:130
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:133

# === Group B: boundaries already answered by a unicode script table =========
#
# isWideRune checks the four script tables (Han, Hangul, Katakana, Hiragana)
# and then nine hardcoded ranges. For these lines, the code point at the mutated
# boundary is ALREADY reported wide by a table, so whether the hardcoded check
# includes it or not cannot change the result. Measured, not assumed: the
# boundary code points below are all inside the table ranges.
#
# The tails that the tables do NOT cover are killed by
# TestToastManager_EveryWideRuneBoundaryIsExact, which is why only the
# table-covered ones remain here.

# internal/ui/toast.go:210 - `r >= 0x1100 && r <= 0x115F` (Hangul Jamo). The whole
# range is inside unicode.Hangul, so both boundary mutants are no-ops. This is
# the one range in the function that is genuinely redundant.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:210

# internal/ui/toast.go:213 and :216 - the head of CJK Radicals Supplement
# (0x2E80) and the tail of 0x3040-0x9FFF. U+2E80 and U+9FFF are both covered by
# a table, so widening or narrowing the check leaves the result unchanged. The
# tails these ranges exist for (U+2E9A, U+3040) are covered separately and their
# boundary mutants are killed.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:213
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:216

# internal/ui/toast.go:219 and :222 - the head of Hangul Syllables (0xAC00) and the
# head of CJK Compatibility Ideographs (0xF900). Both heads are already reported
# wide by unicode.Hangul and unicode.Han respectively.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:219
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:222

# internal/ui/toast.go:234 - the head of CJK Ext B (0x20000). U+20000 falls inside
# the table-covered part of the range; the code points only this check reaches
# (U+2A6E0 onward) have their own coverage in the boundary test.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:234

# === Group C: one accepted, documented risk ================================
#
# internal/ui/toast.go:78 - `now.Sub(toast.Created) < toast.Duration` -> `<=`.
#
# This is NOT provably equivalent: at exactly toast.Duration elapsed, the mutant
# keeps a toast the original expires. It is accepted rather than equivalent
# because the boundary is unreachable without controlling the clock: the test
# would have to make elapsed time exactly equal the duration, which means
# sleeping for a precisely-known duration and racing the scheduler. Every
# measurable duration is either clearly under or clearly over.
#
# Accepted because the real-world effect is nil: Duration is set to 3s by Show()
# and the risk window is a single instant at the 3.000s mark, where keeping a
# toast for one more frame is not a defect. The clock-dependent behaviour itself
# IS covered by TestToastManager_ExpiryComparisonIsStrict, which kills the
# negation variant.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:78
